rt and so really you can think of  this as a lot of the previous research  is focused on this relationship just  maximizing cover crop bomb a square as  we've shifted the focus over here to par  transmittance or shading and what we  found both in the low end high density  treatments as well as all the other  treatments I couldn't discuss was that  in fact shading was a much better  predictor of common ragweed so you can  see here we definitely had higher  r-squared values when we look at this  relationship and what's interesting to  note is as we saw with the low density  in that high here that over yielding and  that lower density treatment high  biomass production doesn't necessarily  result in greater shading so it  definitely suggests to us that we really  need to focus in on shading to really  tackle this issue of early emerging  weeds like common ragweed in terms of  soybean yield we actually saw no  differences between feeding ratio or  management treatments which was  surprising given the differences in  cover crop biomass as well as weed  biomass and on average we got about 40  bushels per acre which is actually the  lowest yields we  across all of our different treatments  and our on farm sites we typically get  45 to 50 bushels which is really  competitive with other organically  produced soybean so tillage based  systems when we compare this to the  intro cultivation we see about eighty  percent of that yield and so that's  definitely a substantial yield loss  there again this is a little bit related  to these were not very productive  treatments for us and of course you need  to factor in the fact that there's going  to be a thirty percent reduction in  diesel fuel on labor with this type of  management so in summary we found that  early season shooting definitely was an  important predictor we're coming driver  of common ragweed bomb ass but these  mixtures of barleans cereal rye might  not be the best way to actually achieve  that enhanced shading we're definitely  encouraged to continue pursuing this  because pre-empting that common ragweed  biomass is just so critical for  addressing these issues for farmers in  the Northeast we also found the irazoo  cultivation was really useful for  controlling that in tirol common ragweed  and you can think of it as a rescue  cultivation and for long term weed  seedbank management this might be a  really useful tool and then this is  actually different than what we'd  expected soybean yield was not higher  when you integrated that high residue  cultivation actually in some ways this  might be a desirable result for us  because what it suggests is if we focus  on cover crop management and so we can  really suppress those weeds with just  standard know till you're not going to  see a yield loss if you don't integrate  high residue cultivation and really for  farmers they're not that interested when  we've gone to grow armies and talked  about this in using high residue  cultivator because the whole purpose or  their whole interest in this is a  reduced tillage system so this idea of  higher education is is is not very  popular and we definitely see a high  potential profitability for this system  like I said if you get these competitive  yields in combination with this  reduction in energy use this is going to  translate into higher profits for  farmers and so with that I just like to  thank particular Chris pelzer Brian  Caldwell on the rest of our lab and all  the folks at the Musgrave research farm  I certainly kind of done this  research without everyone's assistance  and support so there's time I'd love to  take some questions thank you this has  been a production of Cornell University  on the web at cornell edu


